import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 625 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 626 | 2026-10-22 | **Keys and staff section 1, rebuilt against the chapters she had checked (Keyboard Patterns, The Musical Alphabet).** Her answer (2026-10-22 07:07): yes to all four proposals, keep the order of the chapters but not necessarily the same, design it one thing at a time so children and adults can learn and try. Now: (1) **Black keys**: 2, 3, 2-3-2-3, then low and high in her words (left = down under the sea, right = up in the sky); her turn starts with the 2 side, then the 3 side, then listening. (2) **Two houses**, eleven small steps: the small house, then C Clara (left door), D her Dog (doghouse between the two black keys), E the Exit; the big house, then F the Front door, G Grandma (inside, loves apples), A her Apple garden (next to her), B the Back door; the key she stands on is solid with the name above it; the black keys stay black in the close-ups; last, both houses together. Her turn rotates three ways: tap the letter, who lives on the marked key (choices from that house), tap where Grandma/the Dog/... lives. (3) **Every key has its place**: every C (Clara at the left door of every small house), one letter at a time, middle C as the ground (up into the sky, down under the sea), twelve keys and seven names, and round again at C after B; her turn: tap every C (first question is always C), tap middle C or the next C up or down, tap every other letter, which name follows B. Left out on purpose (her answer 4): the lighthouse and counting the 88 keys. The 'left door / inside / right door' wording of the earlier draft is replaced by her characters. For review: the characters are words only (the pen drawings in the chapter and the WhoLivesHere pad are not used yet; reuse is her call). Built by `patch634.py` (+ `kq.jsx`) (`t640`). Opened the five lessons at 390 px in English and Korean, every step, both kinds of answer: no console errors. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
